# AI PRD Generator Engineering Rules

## 1) TypeScript Is Mandatory

- All new code must be written in TypeScript (`.ts` / `.tsx`).
- `any` is forbidden unless there is a documented and reviewed exception.
- Prefer explicit interfaces/types for domain models, agent inputs, and outputs.
- Public functions must have clear input and return types.
- Runtime data from external sources (API/file upload/LLM response) must be validated before use.

## 2) Business Logic Separation

- Never place business logic directly in page-level components.
- Pages and UI components should focus on rendering and user interaction only.
- Put domain logic in `src/lib/` or feature-specific service modules.
- AI workflow orchestration must live in agent/service layers, not in `src/app/**/page.tsx`.
- For complex UI state, move reusable behavior to custom hooks.

## 3) Styling Standards

- Tailwind CSS is the first and default styling solution.
- Avoid ad-hoc CSS files unless a Tailwind-first approach is not feasible.
- Prefer utility classes and design tokens over hard-coded colors or spacing.
- Use `shadcn/ui` components as base primitives before creating custom alternatives.
- Keep dark mode as the default visual mode for this project.

## 4) Component Architecture

- Keep components small, composable, and single-purpose.
- Shared UI primitives belong in `src/components/ui`.
- Feature components should live close to their feature domain.
- Avoid prop drilling across many levels; use composition or scoped state patterns.

## 5) AI Agent Layer Conventions

- Agent responsibilities must remain isolated:
  - `researcher`: data cleaning and sentiment correction
  - `analyst`: pain-point clustering and insight extraction
  - `pm`: PRD generation and formatting
- Agent modules must expose typed contracts for input/output payloads.
- Prompt templates and response parsers must be versioned and testable.
- Never mix prompt-building logic with React presentation components.

## 6) Data and API Practices

- Define typed DTOs for all internal API boundaries.
- Never trust client input; validate and sanitize at the boundary.
- Handle API errors explicitly; do not silently swallow exceptions.
- Keep side effects (network, storage, external SDK calls) in dedicated services.

## 7) Quality Gate

- New features should include at least basic tests for critical paths.
- Linting and type checks must pass before merge.
- Refactors must preserve behavior and update affected types.
- Prefer incremental, reviewable changes over large mixed commits.

## 8) Naming and Readability

- Use clear, intention-revealing names for files, symbols, and variables.
- Avoid abbreviations unless they are domain-standard.
- Add concise comments only where logic is non-obvious.
- Keep functions focused; split when one function handles multiple responsibilities.

